<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Solms-Braunfels</h1><p class="st-meta">1258 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Braunfels</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hessian county on the Lahn ruled from Braunfels castle, whose family later sponsored the German settlement of New Braunfels in Texas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of Solms, named after a stream near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">Wetzlar</a>, divided their lands around 1258. One line took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braunfels">Braunfels</a> castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> as its seat and became the counts of Solms-Braunfels, ruling lands between Wetzlar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a>, held directly from the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was small and rural, with farming, forests and some iron mining. Its counts, often serving as officers in foreign armies, inherited other Solms lands such as Greifenstein and Hungen. They adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Reformed Protestant</a> faith. In 1742 the head of the family, Friedrich Wilhelm, was raised to the rank of imperial prince.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Solms-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braunfels">Braunfels</a> was mediatised in 1806 and divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Hesse-Darmstadt</a>, with the Nassau part passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815. In 1845 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-carl-of-solms-braunfels">Prince Carl of Solms-Braunfels</a> led German settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-braunfels">New Braunfels</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-solms-braunfels.jpg" alt="County of Solms-Braunfels" width="300" height="336"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen-Solms-Braunfels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1258 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Braunfels</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Friedrich Wilhelm of Solms-Braunfels</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Solms-Braunfels, Solms</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=county-of-solms-braunfels" aria-label="County of Solms-Braunfels on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4871 423 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Solms-Braunfels lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5191 679L5229 694M5191 679L5189 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5189 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5191 679h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5191 679h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Braunfels. <a href="/atlas?year=1532&amp;focus=county-of-solms-braunfels">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
